{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's causal mechanism ('Distributed execution' / 'Expert-as-Process') explains at most why a checklist might reduce variance relative to a single expert's intuition, but it never supplies a causal account of the distinguishing evidence the problem demands: how, within a single evaluation, one tells a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. The mechanism assumes the checklist is a 'compiled version of expert intuition' and that consistency is the driver of reliability, but consistency can produce the same observed correct answers whether the panel is genuinely tracking the underlying task structure or merely following surface cues that correlate with the answer in that evaluation. No causal pathway is offered for how a single evaluation would separate these — e.g., what error profile, item-level disagreement pattern, or perturbation response would be causally expected under genuine reliability versus under coincidental compliance. The counterconditions are exclusions, not distinguishing evidence. Thus the target fails the problem's second requirement while asserting it has answered it.","counterexample":["A panel of literate non-experts uses a checklist authored by a domain expert and produces highly consistent, mostly correct answers on an evaluation. By the target's own mechanism (checklist = compiled intuition; consistency > person) this would count as a reliability gain. Yet the checklist may encode surface proxies that happen to correlate with the verifiable outcome only in the sampled items; the same consistency would appear under mere compliance. The target provides no causal mechanism within a single evaluation to tell these apart, despite the problem explicitly requiring it."],"target":"f4465ec8996a9a8d483a005576cb58ca3f0072a9d7ebe282cc5fb37fb69b008f"}]}